  "_source": "Deterministic parse of raw Telair PBX CSV exports (ExportCallRecords, InboundCalls_Detailed, CallQueue_Detailed per queue) via scripts/parse_call_logs.py, replacing the earlier PDF-text-extracted version first committed for this date (the OneDrive raw-source folders had this date's files ready on the very next daily routine firing, so this date now matches 2026-10-06's own raw-pipeline sourcing). All figures cross-checked against Daily Operations Report - 7th October 2026.pdf and matched within 1 call (281/234 PDF vs 282/235 here — the PDF's own cover rounds slightly differently than a full raw-CSV dedup count, same small gap seen between report and source on other dates).",
  "reportTitle": "Daily Operations Report - 7th October 2026",
  "_summaryVsGrandTotalNote": "summary (235 accepted) is 2 short of grandTotal's own sum (233) inverted — actually grandTotal (233) is 2 short of summary (235): 2 of the 235 accepted calls aren't attributable to any of the 4 named queues at all (a different reception line, same pattern documented on 2026-10-06, where the gap was 2 calls too). Missed matches exactly both ways (47=47).",
  "_tenDayTrendNote": "accepted/missed/overallAvgWaitSec all chain-matched 2026-10-06's own committed trend exactly at positions 0-8; position 9 is this date's own raw-CSV-derived figure, confirmed against the PDF's own cover (83% answer rate within rounding) and its own 10-day wait-time chart exactly (43 sec, to the second) once scripts/parse_call_logs.py's average-wait bug (see _avgWaitFixNote) was fixed.",
  "_queuesNote": "accepted/missed for all four queues chain-match 2026-10-06's own committed data exactly at positions 0-8 (Appointments position 8 = 207, matching the already-documented 1-call residual from 2026-10-06, not a new issue). avgWaitSec now matches the PDF's own per-queue 10-day charts exactly at position 9 (Appointments 43, General Enquiries 44, Canberra Vasectomy 3) — see _avgWaitFixNote.",
  "_avgWaitFixNote": "FIXED 2026-10-07: scripts/parse_call_logs.py computed per-queue and overall average wait time using CallTimeToAnswer, on the assumption (stated in the script's own prior comment) that this matched the rendered report's 'Average Wait Time' charts the same way CallTimeToAnswer differs from UserTimeToAnswer for the single 'longest wait' KPI. That assumption was never independently verified for the average and turned out to be backwards: mean(UserTimeToAnswer) matches this report's own 10-day average-wait charts exactly (43/44/3 sec for Appointments/General Enquiries/Canberra Vasectomy, and 43 sec overall — all confirmed to the second against raw CSV data), while mean(CallTimeToAnswer) read roughly 3x too high (138/147/242/141 sec). The script now uses UserTimeToAnswer for both average and longest wait. Per the user's 'just fix moving forward' instruction, this is NOT applied retroactively — every already-committed date's own avgWaitSec/overallAvgWaitSec fields (all computed by the old, now-known-wrong code) are left as they were."
